BOX MODEL IN CSS:
i.Height
ii.Width
iii.Border
iv.Padding
v.Margin

i.Height:
By default, its set's the content area height of the element.

div {
    height:100px;
}

ii.Width:
By default, its set's the content area Width of the element.

div {
    Width:100px;
}

iii.Border:
Used to set an elementborder

.border-Width
.border-style
.border-color

border shorthand:
Width | style | color 

div{
    border:2px solid green;
}

Border Sides:
To conterol an individual side of the box.

border-left
border-rigt
border-top
border-bottom

div {
    border-left-width:2px;
    border-top:2px solid green;
}

Border radius:
Used to round the corners of an element's outer border edge

div{
    border-radius:50px;
    border-top-right-radius:15px;
}

div{
    border-radius:50%;
    
}

iv.Padding:

padding-left
padding-right
padding-top
padding-bottom


div{
    padding-left:24px;
}

Padding Shorthand:

#1.for all 4 side:
    padding:5px;

#2.top & buttom | left & right:
    padding:1px 2px;

#3.top |  left & right | buttom:
    padding:1px 2px 3px;

#4. top|right|bottom|left (clockwise):
    padding:1px 2px 3px 4px;

v.Margin:
To set  spacing on the outside the border

    margin-left:
    margin-right:
    margin-top:
    margin-buttom:

    div{
        margin-left:50px;
    }


Margin Shorthand:

#1.for all 4 side:
    margin:5px;

#2.top & buttom | left & right:
    margin:1px 2px;

#3.top |  left & right | buttom:
    margin:1px 2px 3px;

#4. top|right|bottom|left (clockwise):
    margin:1px 2px 3px 4px;



INLINE VS BLOCK:

Block Elements:
Takes up the full weidth available (whole block)
Start from new line.

Inline Elements:
Takes up only necessary weidth.
Don't start from new line.


DISPLAY:
It sets the whether an  element is treated as a inline or block element and the layout used
for children

    display:inline;
    display:block;
    display:inline-block;(behave as inline element and style apply like block element)
    display:none;(to hide contenet from screen)


    inline(span):
    -->padding & margin set left & right only. Height and weidth are not set.
    -->Do not start on a new line 
    -->Only use as much horizontal space as required by the content. 
            Do not accept width and height CSS properties 
    -->Margins will work horizontally, but not vertically 
    -->Padding works on all sides, but the top and bottom may overlap other elements. 
   

    block(div):
    -->Always start on a new line 
    -->Take up as much horizontal space as possible 
            (the full width of the container or browser window if there is no container) 
    -->Will respect width and height CSS properties 
    -->Horizontal and vertical margins both work 

PERSENTAGE(%):
    It is often used to define a size as relative to an element's parent .

    eg:
    width:33.33%; // relative to the parent.
    margin-left:50%; // relative to parent size.


EN:
em set->parent/self
Related to:
Font size of the parent,in the case of typographical properties like font-size,and 
font size of the element itself,in the case of other properties like width.

eg;
<div id= "outer">
<div id="inner"></div>
</div>

font-size of outer is 10 px,
if you set the font size of inner is 3em then is 3 x font-size of outer
if you set the padding 1em of inner then padding equal to font-size of inner


DRAWBACK OF EM:
Snowball effect in nested elements.


REM(ROOT EM):
>set ATG root element
unit:rem
Related to:Font size of the root element.

->For an HTML document, the root element is <html>. So,rem will work according to that.here 
    font-size is changing based on <html>